  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Enamel Network",
    "overview": "A career is a transit network you can read. Each stack is a line, each employer or project a station, each year a band across the ground. The map is generated from the site's data, so the 3D model, the flat diagram and the station links cannot disagree with one another or with the content. The visual system is the working vocabulary of a transit authority: pale enamel ground, black signs, seven saturated line colours, white station capsules, round lettered bullets.\n\nDensity is high on the map and in the record tables, then loosens into full-bleed colour posters and display-scale figures. Hierarchy comes from size, width and rules, not from containers. Night is the same system underground: every role survives the switch, with brighter line colours, a black sign and a dark plate.\n\nThe enamel is a direction the build has only partly reached. Tracks and stations are extruded, bevelled, clearcoated solids on a lit plate. The plate shows sheen but no visible panel edge, because it is a 1600-unit plane running past every frame. The sign's enamel finish is only a top-lit gradient. Both are open items, not finished material.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Seven line colours that each mean one stack, in every medium the site uses.",
      "Black signs carrying the name, navigation and the end of every page.",
      "One unbroken route line and a sticky train down each long page.",
      "Archivo at slightly extended widths throughout; Geist Mono only for data.",
      "A real-time model that loads after a flat SVG of the same map, which stays complete without WebGL or motion."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Line Means A Stack Rule",
        "body": "A line colour appears only where that stack or its project is the subject: the track, its bullet, its route on a project page, its poster field. Colour is never chosen for variety.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Lit Line Rule",
        "body": "Choosing a line keeps it at full colour and drains the rest: unpressed bullets drop to 45% opacity, flat map lines to 14%, and 3D tracks sink and blend most of the way to the Drained colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Career Inversion Rule",
        "body": "Career is whatever the ink is, so it flips with its surface: inside a sign or ticket line C becomes light with dark text, and in lists on the ground it follows ink.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Signage Width Rule",
        "body": "Display and headline settings run at widths from 104% to 110% with negative tracking that grows with size. Body stays at normal width.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Is Data Rule",
        "body": "Geist Mono is for dates, counts, keys and code. Proof figures such as \"1,000+ users\" are display Archivo with tabular numerals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Map Slant Rule",
        "body": "On the wide map every name sits at one angle (-34deg), on one of two opposing sides of its stop, stroked with a 3px ground halo. If no clean position exists, a major name moves clear on a 1.5px leader and a minor one waits for hover. Tall and on-end views set names horizontally.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Route Rule",
        "body": "Long pages hang from one unbroken track at the 44px rail (15px on phones), 8px wide (6px on phones). Each section title is a stop on it, and a white train rides at 56vh of the viewport. Project pages run their route in the project's first line colour; other pages run in Career.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Clear Space Rule",
        "body": "The sign measures its own top edge and reports it, and the camera, flat map and label placement all frame the map inside what remains. Nothing on the map sits under a sign.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hung Objects Rule",
        "body": "Only objects that hang over something cast a soft shadow: frames, the ticket, the train. Signs, rows, fields and buttons do not.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Round Means Network Rule",
        "body": "A full circle or capsule marks a part of the network: a line, a station or a train. A generic control does not get one.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use a line colour only for its stack, and fill a whole section with it when that stack's work is the subject.",
      "Do run body text and signage in Archivo, and save Geist Mono for dates, counts, keys and code.",
      "Do keep the flat map complete: every station link and every page must work with WebGL or motion off.",
      "Do generate anything drawn on the map from `data/network.ts` so the model stays an accurate index.",
      "Do separate content with 1.5px rules and open space.",
      "Do hang screenshots in Frame Black mounts with the Hung Frame shadow.",
      "Do end each page on the black terminus with the route to Contact."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't give text content a card, tinted tile or bordered panel. The only floating text panel is the map's ticket.",
      "Don't use circles or capsules for generic controls, or square off a bullet or station.",
      "Don't add a small label above a heading. Above a name there may be a working control, such as the picker or the back link, and nothing else.",
      "Don't set labels in uppercase or wide tracking; the system is sentence case throughout.",
      "Don't add a soft shadow to signs, rows, buttons or fields.",
      "Don't add ornamental map marks with no station or year behind them.",
      "Don't treat the sign's gradient or the plate's sheen as finished enamel to reproduce on new surfaces."
    ]
  }
}
